>	Actually, if you want to use drug laws and forfeiture laws as
>applicable laws, the police have been able to seize property with as
>few as 1 violations -- if they have reason to believe that the property
>is used regularly for drug transactions -- *regardless* of the
>owner's knowledge.  

There have been cases involving this, yes.  But recently the law has gotten
a little more intelligent, and has been asking the second-level question --
is the owner innocent of knowing whether or not this is going on.

See the federal procedures for expedited release of some items (Yes, this
section really does exist in the forfeiture codes now.  It was added after a
few planes that were owned by large companies got seized :-)

However, if you are aware of the problem and do nothing (ie: you lease a
plane to "Karl's Coccaine Runners, Inc.") you're screwed.

>	Of course harping on our previous discussion, the fact that 
>a USENET administrator removed 1 group because of copyright violations
>means that the owner was aware of the potential copyright violations
>and in effect took some steps to prevent that.  This would deny the
>defense that they should be considered "as common carriers" for
>purposes of carrying material, since they are censoring based on
>content.

The common carrier defense has <NEVER> been tried in court.  I feel that it
would not stand for my site and most others, since ALL news admins (with a
few exceptions for monster sites such as UUNET) make content decisions on the
groups they carry.  Further, UUNET is a very special case -- they are actually
a registered ESP (from what I've been told), and thus are IMMUNE to content
problems.  However, they had to file for that with the FCC, and I bet it
comes with some regulatory problems as well.

"Common carrier" is a VERY specific term.  You would not want to operate a
site under those rules.  Among other things, to be a common carrier you have
to:

1)	File tariffs with the regulatory authorities stating your rates, and
	those authorities have the right to <deny> those rates as unreasonable.

2)	Provide service to all comers on equal terms, without exception.
	That is, you CANNOT refuse to service anyone, or cut special deals
	with anyone (even if based on sound business principles), or deny
	service to someone (for any reason whatsoever other than inability
	to pay).

3)	You must exercise <zero> control, and <zero> monitoring, over what
	goes on via your system -- except for "quality of service" checks.

There's more, but I think you're getting the idea.  Common carrier status
is NOT a panacea, and not something that most sites would accept under any
set of conditions.

>	The reason for *why* a site doesn't carry a group must be 
>considered.  If I was a common carrier phone company, I am not allowed
>to deny service based on content -- or even monitor content, but
>if a customer approaches me and says they need 1 million lines during
>peak usage -- and need it guaranteed that they will be available, I
>could, as a common carrier still deny them service on the basis that
>I don't have the capacity to serve them.
>
>	The same can be true of USENET groups.  If I censor a group 
>because of copyright violations, that is very different than if I
>choose not to carry a group because my equipment doesn't have the
>capacity to carry that group.

Nope.  That's never been tried, and I bet its a losing argument.  

In ALL cases you choose what to carry and what not to based on content.
Without exception.  You can try to paint this in any color you like, but its
fact.  A fact that won't be lost on the court.

In fact, if you do censor based on "capacity" the question is going to be
raised if that is <really> the issue.  After all, you could drop 10 other
groups instead of abpe, could you not?  So is this <really> just a capacity
issue?  Nope, its not.

The real issue in every case is that you feel the marginal value does not
exceed the marginal cost.  Therefore you don't carry the group.  Period.

In the case of the admin who believes there is a copyright problem the
marginal cost includes the cost of defending against lawsuits which might
arise.  In the case of a high-volume group it includes the cost of the disk
drives.  IN EVERY CASE THIS IS A CONTENT DECISION, AND IS BASED ON THE SAME
ACTUAL FORMULA. 

Only the values you assign to the sides of the equation differ.

