YORK COLLEGE STUDENTS FIGHT RACIST DISCIPLINE

By David Perez
New York

There's growing support for three students at York College who
face disciplinary action up to and including expulsion because
they took a stand against racism.

Jose Cinque, Quentin Walcott and Adrian Charles are all members
of York's Political Science Club. In October they invited African
American Studies Professor Leonard Jeffries, among others, to
speak at a campus political forum set for Nov. 3.

Jeffries is an outspoken defender of the African American
community. He was stripped of his position as head of the Black
Studies Department at City College in 1991, due to a racist
backlash over a speech he gave in Albany describing the
historical oppression of Black people.

Widespread student and community support for Jeffries, however,
forced a federal judge to reinstate him.

Forum proceeds, but so do charges

As soon as the York College Political Science Club decided to
invite Jeffries to speak, an immediate struggle ensued. When
school administrators heard about the event, they canceled it and
leveled charges against the students for "inappropriate
behavior."

In response, York students charged the college administration
with racism and violating their right to organize. As a result of
their struggle, the administration was compelled to let the
activity proceed.

York College President Josephine Davis has denied the school was
out to censor the students. However, New York Newsday reported
Dec. 7 that one professor "who spoke on condition of anonymity
said Davis and her administration have disliked Jeffries since
the last time he spoke at York two years ago."

Richard Boris, a York professor and the faculty union president,
added, "The administration used every ruse in the book to prevent
Jeffries from speaking."

The students won an initial victory Dec. 16, when a disciplinary
hearing for that day was postponed. It was clear the struggle on
the campus made the administration think twice about proceeding
against the students.

But the school has yet to dismiss the charges. The hearing will
be rescheduled.

Administrators may hope that, with most students off campus for
the holiday break, they can proceed with their racist campaign
against the Political Science Club members. But the students'
supporters say they won't let up. They are organizing to defend
the three--and calling on Davis to resign.

A support meeting is set for Dec. 29. Those who want to attend
can call the Political Science Club at (718) 262-2255 for
details.

