Let me see if I understand this correctly:

We (the readers of a.b.p.e) are concerned about the possibility of copyright
holders (Playboy, Penthouse, others) getting upset that some of their material
is being distributed through Usenet.  It concerns us because, laws of this
land being what they are, they could, if they so chose, cause a major stink
for us.  In the process, we could lose not only those pictures that are
actually upsetting the copyright holders (which would be understandable)
but also others, either through general fear or pruning of the a.b.p.e
distribution tree or whatever (which would be a shame.)
It also concerns us, and rightly so, because we just don't know how such
a case would actually turn out.  Posters have cited precedences for both
sides of the case: Ann Landers, Event Horizons, maybe others I missed...
the bottom line, however, is that the only thing we could really be sure of
is that it would get real ugly before the matter was settled.

Thats how I understand the discussion so far.  Am I close?

Now, I didn't write all this just to waste bandwidth checking my assumptions.
I actually have a suggestion.  

Perhaps someone should ask Playboy Enterprises, et al, how they feel?
A well worded query adressed to the editorial staff of the companies in 
question may actually get an intelligent response, one that could guide
us in our self-police-ing.

Sample off the top of my head:

Dear Playboy,
I am one of many readers of the Usenet newsgroup alt.binaries.pictures.erotica,
[brief explanation of Usenet, a.b.p.e, and this group's charter]
We on a.b.p.e are concerned about a situation that, if handled poorly, could
cause much heartache for many.  
  Some of the pictures distributed through this group originate from your 
magazine.  We know that PEI holds copyright on these pictures, and that
leads us to ask ourselves, "Will PEI feel threatened by our use of their 
material?  Would they feel so threatened as to begin legal proceedings?
Can we not investigate the matter so as to find a course of action
which would be agreeable to most, if not all?"
  The people who post pictures to our group are private citiczens all over
the world.  While we cannot censor their input (and wouldn't if we could)
we can make known to them how the group as a whole feels about the matter.
We prefer to police ourselves - to enjoy our freedoms while exercising 
enough restraint that we do not offend other's rights.

[etc. etc. etc....]

End of Sample.

I understand that the pictorials in these magazines are one of their major
selling points, but not the only one.  There do exist many readers who
purchase these magazines for the rest of the material also.  From a
profit/loss standpoint, then, I believe the question is: How much does
a publisher stand to lose from having his/her pictures distributed in this
manner, and hwo much does he/she stand to gain from the free advertising?
This, to me, seems to be the the question a publisher will ask when considering
which course of action to take.  It is a question that only the publisher
can answer.

Is it not therefore in our own best interests to have a publisher direct that
answer to us, when we choose to ask it and are prepared to act on it, rather
than to his/her legal department?

I use Playboy Magazine as an example because, quite honestly, I am
not as familiar with the other publications out there.  I am familiar
with PEI's magazine because I am a subscriber who reads almost every issue
cover to cover (yes, articles and pictures both :).  One of the things
that strikes me as relevant here is that PEI has a long standing reputation
for defending freedom of expression.  

IF we do not post so much of their material that they feel we are infringing
   upon their rights  (either because we currently don't post too much or
   because, at their request, we voluntarily reduce or eliminate their
   copyrighted material from our group)

IF we are capable of self regulation and other such rational behavior

THEN might these publishers, with their large legal departments which have 
   been through first amendment cases time and time again, not only see
   us as a group with which they can coexist, but also as a group whose
   rights of expression are also worth defending?

Bottom line: it's entirely too possible that at any time some moralistic
agency or another could decide to come down on a.b.p.e, for any reason
you care to mention.  No, I don't think for a minute that they might be
able to shut down the entire Internet... not likely at all, but they
could raise a heck of a stink, and cause a lot of hate and discontent.
This scares me because I, for one, have no idea what we, collectively 
or individually, could do about it.  It would be real nice to have a 
big legal friend in such circumstances, especially one which has been
through all this before.

Perhaps I am being a bit naive, but I think it's worth a try.

