In article <CIuBMJ.7uJ@odin.corp.sgi.com> lear@yeager.corp.sgi.com (Eliot Lear) writes:
>I have severe problems with this logic, visa vi the First Amendment.
>You are, in affect (if not in purpose- I don't believe that) requiring
>a ban on what amounts to a topic-based class of communication- a
>newsgroup.  If you argue that this does not actually prevent
>communication, then there is no purpose in removing the group.

I agree with the problems.  The problem is that the de facto principle topic for
a group like supermodels, and several of the others, is "pictures owned by
other people."   The minority subtopic, "pictures legal to post" is the one
hurt by this.

The primary de facto topic has no 1st amendment protection.  The 1st amendment
only protects the right to publish your own expressions, not those of others
without their permission.

THese groups should not be banned, they should be cleaned up (legally -- I
presume that some will be kept nice and "dirty" in the other sense).  The part
that people don't want to hear is that if they are not cleaned up, then
knowingly keeping a repository of copyrighted information on your server is
something that could put you at risk.

People complain that this is just my opinion, but the only part that's really
opinion here is the last sentence.

Maybe I will be a pariah, but I think that knowingly keeping a server full
of pirated information *is* something that should put you at risk. I
don't think it should be legal.   That is what they confiscate BBSs for,
so it's more than my opinion that this is illegal.

I think you have two choices.  The law does not require you to remove the
group, I may have gone too far in saying that.   What I meant to say is
that the law requires you not keep the pirated information.  The implication
has always been that nobody has the time to sort out the pirated from the
legal, and so they only likely solution is to remove the whole group to
stay safe.   My real preference would be for everybody to sort through so
that the rights of those posting legal postings not be infringed.

But I doubt admins want to do that.

What is unknown is whether the defence of "I know there is lots of pirated
stuff there, but I can't possibly have time to go and find it and delete it"
would be accepted or not.
