Brad:

Thanks for your posting.  I'm glad you clarified your position about
what exactly needs to go.  We agree that pirated material ought not be
placed on the net.  We agree that those who place it on the net ought
to be held responsible in the appropriate jurisdiction.  We agree that
those made aware of pirated material on their systems who do nothing
are probably putting themselves at risk.

We disagree as to what it means to be made aware of such information,
and what specific remedies ought to be required of administrators.  I
claim that the shots in alt.binaries.pictures.supermodels were there
before alt.binaries.pictures, and would be there after we deleted such
newsgroups.  For that simple reason, deleting the newsgroup will *not*
remedy a violation.  Instead, specific violations must be pointed out
and action taken to remove the offending article(s)from a news system.
I am quite willing to work with copyright holders to protect their
rights on my systems.  Within the limits of my abilities as defined by
my roll at my place of employment, that includes helping them trace
violations to their source, and if necessary prosecute (read: I would
not enjoy a fishing expedition).

However, I cannot scour my disks looking for violations all day long.
To do so would require me to take my marbles and go home.  It could
put concerns such as UUNET out of the business of news (and possibly
mail) distribution.  In other words, IDOTNP(tm).

