>many people here actually do believe that?  I believe that posters of 
>infringing materials should be found, and told to stop, with legal threats
>if necessary from the copyright holders. 
>
>I DON'T think self-appointed netcops should be shutting down reception at
>their local sites.   

Oh, so now you have the right to dictate what I spend <MY> resources on?
You think that we ought to all listen to you dictate to us what we should
carry in the newsgroup arena, but you shouldn't listen to us?  Did I get
that right?

Where do you get off with <that> attitude?

> Nor should anyone be encouraging censorship of any
>forum, even indirect censorship, even when the forum is a hotbed of 
>alleged "illegal" activity. 

Let me draw another parallel.

A given bar has a large dope-dealing contingent.  The owner refuses to eject
the dope dealers from the bar, saying that "he's not going to infringe on
their right to be here", even though what they're doing is illegal.

Should the bar be closed down?  Why or why not?

>You incessantly chatter about going to jail for it.  Feh. 
>The moral argument that it is theft of someone else's property at least
>carries some weight.  Smoking dope and prostitution and lots of other things
>are illegal, too, but shouldn't be.  Harping about the "illegality" of
>copyright violation certainly doesn't lend your arguments any credibility. 
>
>Stealing "intellectual property," or whatever you want to call it, (ie
>copyright violation) is WRONG.  And it ought to be stopped at the SOURCE,
>not at the sites "lucky" enough to have vigilante netcops who cut off
>the resource because of a few bad apples.  

A FEW bad apples?  Would you care to find the good ones and show us some
comparisons of the counts?  Use alt.binaries.pictures.supermodels for your
test.  That's one of the ones which I have removed here, but still check out
on other systems from time to time.

If the entire barrel of apples is rotten you toss it.  If there is one bad
apple you remove the one.  But abps is not a case of a "few" bad apples --
it is a case of <all> bad apples.

ABPE is not "all" bad apples.  Just something like 30-40% confirmed, and
probably somewhere around 70-80% suspected.

>If a site admin is filing charges with due process proceedings 
>for individuals on his or her system who post copyrighted materials, I don't
>have a problem with it.  You're not any of the above.  You're just blowing
>hot air.  And you responded to my post with a knee-jerk insinuating question
>because I dared to criticize a fairy tale scenario that tried to equate
>copyright violation with bank robbery.
>
>"a.b.p.e" is not to blame for those posts.  "Usenet" is not to blame for
>those posts.  "Society" is not to blame for those posts.  The *individuals*
>who are posting scans of copyrighted pictures are to blame, and only they
>should suffer any inconvenience.  

You're wrong Christopher.  Try that argument in a real setting (ie: outside
of an ".edu" site) and you'll find out how far it goes.  Like nowhere.

The society which comprises the net IS to blame for these posts, because
they are not only tolerated but <encouraged> by people who want to look at
the pictures, stolen or not.  

If you have a streetcorner which you own, and you have people coming there
to sell property on the lot all the time, and it happens that 99% of that
property is stolen, and people have told you that the property is stolen,
what do you think will happen to your streetcorner market?

It will be closed down.

If I find people posting material from here which appears to be stolen you
can bet I'm going to insist on that person producing proof of their right to
make the copies.  If they can't provide it then they've got a problem.

I don't need to have someone thrown in jail for copying 1-2-3 to know that
posting archives with 1-2-3 pieces in it constitutes copyright violation.
Likewise, I don't need to have someone convicted of stealing pictures to
know that scanning in the Playmate of the Year is a copyright violation
unless the person doing the scanning can produce something that contravenes
the masthead of the magazine -- and in writing, since that's how the 
masthead is produced.

