Karl Denninger writes:
 
>>The above is a substantial misrepresentation of the facts.
>>Neither Neidorf nor Riggs, Darden, and Grant, were charged with
>>or convicted of copyright violations. Riggs, Darden, and Grant
>>initially came under investigation after a juvenile was busted
>>for credit card fraud. Their crime was a "hacking" case, not a
>>"piracy" case.
 
    >What was it that they had and trafficked in that was copyrighted?
    >What started this entire thing?  The E911 document.
 
What started the entire thing was illicit computer access.
Please read the original indictments, press releases, and other
material and tell us how you interpret the cases as "piracy."
 
>> Neidorf was not accused of "hacking." He was
>>indicted under 18 USC 1343 (wire fraud) and 18 USC 2314 (ISTSP).
>>He did not "get off." The government halted the prosecution
>>before it completed its opening argument when it realized that it
>>did not have a case.
 
    >Let's talk about the real reason the government halted its case.
    >That is, they halted the prosecution because <the document which
    >he was alleged to have trafficked in was worth $25.00, and they
    >had materially misrepresented that cost at $80K+>.
 
Which makes the point that Neidorf was not involved in "piracy" or
copyright violations. If you've any doubt about what the case was
about, read the opening statements of the prosecution and defense
published in CuD a few years ago.
 
    >What was the "wire fraud" charge all about?  Are you aware that
    >transmitting stolen material over the phone lines can be
    >construed as "wire fraud"?  Posting access "codez" is the same
    >thing, is it not?  Those access codes are NOT yours.
 
I'm fully aware of the charges and the laws underlying them.
Posting access codes is not a "piracy" issue. Neither Len Rose
nor Craig Neidorf were accused of posting codes. Could you clarify
your point?
 
>>Len originally faced a five count indictment under 18 USC
>>1030(a)(6), computer fraud; 18 USC 2314, ISTSP; and 18 USC 2, aiding
>>and abetting. The indictments and other public documents are readily
>>available and on-line.  Len's case was more complicated than the
>>others, and both his conviction and sentence suggested that he was
>>harshly treated.
 
    >Yes, he was harshly treated.  He was also in trouble <twice> from
    >what I remember.  Again, wire and computer fraud can be committed
    >when you send stolen electronic material over the telephone
    >lines.  This is the insidious nature of these laws; they happen
    >to turn information theft into VERY serious crimes.  This is part
    >of the reason that I am very much against anything which looks
    >like organized information theft via the net.
 
When busted in 1990, Len had only one previous run-in with the
law on an offense unrelated to computer abuse. Your point about the
misuse of law by government officials should be re-emphasized:
Prosecutors who act in ignorance or in bad faith can create
considerable disrespect for law. But, let's not confuse terms
like "theft," "copyright violations," or "proprietary information."
 
>>The folks above, along with many others, were caught up in the first
>>"hacker crackdown" in 1989/1990. Piracy was not the issue.
 
    >Not true.  Piracy is frequently prosecuted as wire fraud.  In
    >fact, it is one of the most effective ways to prosecute it from
    >the government's point of view.
 
Can you name a case in which software piracy has been prosecuted
as wire fraud?  I can't think of one off-hand. In fact, prosecutions
for piracy are rare, even under the revison of the federal
"anti-piracy" statute. The point remains that the cases previously
identified as "piracy" cases (Neidorf, Rose, the "Atlanta 3") were no
more piracy cases than Willy Sutton's escapades were "automobile
crimes."
 
   >>Contrary to earlier suggestions, none of the alleged offenses in any
   >>of the above cases were net-related. Collapsing these and other
   >>disparate cases together to make a case against net piracy seems a
   >>rather confused exercise.
 
    >I disagree.  They are certainly net related.  What do you think
    >was used to transport the files around in the E911 cases?  What
    >was used to transport the files in the R&E case?
    >
    >What do you use when you dial into the net, or when you access
    >the wider Internet itself?
    >
    >What was used to transport the files in the R&E case?
    >
    >What do you use when you dial into the net, or when you access
    >the wider Internet itself?
 
Perhaps I'm simply not understanding your point. Could you clarify it?
In the E911 cases, the nets were used, and in the R&E case, dialins to
the BBS were used. This doesn't make it net-related except in a broad
and rather meaningless sense.  But, perhaps I'm missing your point. If
you're suggesting that the fact that use of telephone lines place
alleged offenders at great risk of creative and more serious
prosecution for what might otherwise be minor offenses, then I'd
agree.  But, if you're trying to expand the definition of "piracy," a
vague and often misused term, perhaps you could clarify.
 
My original intent here was merely to clarify the misconception of the
Phrack/Rose/LOD cases, which were not "piracy" indictments. Using those
cases to illustrate the dangers of piracy grossly distorts what the
cases were about.
 
Jim Thomas
 


