To your surprise?  Why?

What would you have an admin do if you created a mail forwarding loop that
was consuming the entire machine's disk resource?  Turn off the server and
deny service to the remainder of the user community?  Because you blew it
and are creating a problem?  

Nope.  Privacy loses in those cases to The Greater Ghod of maintaining 
service at acceptable levels for everyone.  In the process of fixing the
problem our admins will try to figure out <why> it broke and advise you.
This usually means looking at the headers, and there is no good way to
prevent seeing the text of a message while doing this.

> Let me say in the specific case I am referring to, nothing whatsoever illegal is going on but the email in question involves
>a personal conversation between a man and woman.

Well, if its general snooping I would condemn it -- loudly.  If it happened
here at MCSNet someone would get in serious trouble, up to and including 
fired, even though it is technically allowed by our user agreement.  There's 
a difference between what we <can> (and must reserve the right to) do, and 
what I'll permit my admins (myself included) to do.

>Unfortunately, the responses I'm getting seem to say that (a) sysadmins often
>read users mail and (b) it's legal.

Depends on what the service agreement says.  <Absent a disclaimer> you are
probably covered by the ECPA and it is NOT legal.  Any site which doesn't
include such a disclaimer is asking for a monstrous lawsuit the first time
they have to go fix something, the admin gets a look at some email in the 
process, and someone gets pissed off and sues.

The site will win those cases, but it will have to fight.  An ECPA disclaimer
keeps you <out> of court, or at least allows an immediate dismissal if someone 
gets it in their mind to bring suit.  It is also good business sense because 
there is no way to control the propagation or activities of the other admins 
in the chain of delivery.  There's no way MCSNet wants to get sued over a 
privacy violation on some <other> site, but it could happen if I allowed 
full ECPA protection to subscribers here.

