>like "theft," "copyright violations," or "proprietary information."

Wait a second.  I disagree that this is "acting in bad faith".  Technology
makes <easy> what used to be difficult.  If you wanted to reproduce a photo
of a nude woman in the old days, you had to either shoot it with a camera
and then reprint, or go to a <lot> of trouble to do a direct reproduction.
Today you can buy a $1000 scanner that will crank out near-perfect
reproductions in digital form and allow you to distribute <millions> of
copies of that work with a push of a button.

What we have done is, by the advance of technology, allowed people to
arbitrarily <destroy> the value of that photograph to the owner.  I believe
that it is proper to have strong legal protections in the event that people
abuse technology to that end.

I believe that the prosecution of someone for theft and wire fraud
(misrepresenting that you have the right to do something, and inducing
others to go along on that basis is <fraud>) is not only proper prosecution
but <good law>.  The only way to keep up with technological advances that 
make it easier to steal is to make the penalties ramp up as the barriers to
committing the act of fraud fall.

This is why stolen credit card numbers and phone card numbers are prosecuted
as vigorously as they are, and why they are considered "wire fraud".  You're
using the phone system to transmit information you have no right to posess 
and use.  Absent those laws the credit card industry is <gone>, as is the
third-party (bill to card) phone industry.  Rampant theft, without the check
and balance of these laws, would make the entire information provision
industry subject to destruction.

Oh, certainly they could go to "one time pins" or something like that.  At 
what cost?  Those who argue that this is the way to protect information
forget that some information, particularly that which is in print now, 
simply cannot be protected in that fashion.

I have heard people argue that they're doing a "service" to the industry by
exposing its weakness.  Bull.  Those people are stealing because they think
they can get away with it.  The law says "no", and I happen to agree with
it on that point.

Are we really wishing for the destruction of the entire print information
industry?  So some people can be happy and scan to their heart's content?

What about source code?  It is just bits on a disk.  Should I be unable to
recover damages if someone steals the proprietary code which makes MCSNet
run the way it does?  That's a <significant> piece of software.  I could
easily make the case that it is worth millions of dollars, in that it 
represents novel and unique ways of managing clusters of machines and the
billing issues that come along with running a public access Internet site.

If someone <does> break in here and get ahold of the material I think I
should be able to prosecute and recover the same amount in damages as if
they had stolen gold from my personal vault.  The law happens to agree with
me at present.

>>>The folks above, along with many others, were caught up in the first
