>In the E911 cases, the nets were used, and in the R&E case, dialins to
>the BBS were used. This doesn't make it net-related except in a broad
>and rather meaningless sense.  But, perhaps I'm missing your point. If
>you're suggesting that the fact that use of telephone lines place
>alleged offenders at great risk of creative and more serious
>prosecution for what might otherwise be minor offenses, then I'd
>agree.  But, if you're trying to expand the definition of "piracy," a
>vague and often misused term, perhaps you could clarify.

The net was used extensively in the E911 cases.  A good part of that took
place in my backyard, so to speak.  

My point is that when someone steals an item of intellectual property, by
whatever means, that fits the definition of piracy.  When you copy Lotus
1-2-3 and give it to a friend you have <stolen> intellectual property and
violated copyright.  That is a case of piracy.

When you break into a computer system and steal a document, <even if it is
worth only $25>, you have committed an act of information piracy.

When you scan a supermodel calender and post the pictures you have committed
an act of piracy, <even if the calendar only cost you $5.00>.

In the second and third cases you could easily get in <real> serious
trouble.  That $25 or $5 sounds like petty theft, doesn't it?  But multiply
the $5.00 by 25,000 Usenet sites and something like 100,000 readers, and all
of a sudden you're talking about <real money>.  And it is the person who is
committing the act of posting and scanning who is ultimately responsible for
that loss.

Unfortunately, it is a principle of civil law that the person bringing suit
will name everyone who can be identified in the hopes of finding someone
with deep enough pockets to pay the judgement.  This is the way it is,
whether we like it or not.  Thus, while you certainly want to go after the
poster, the fact is that <large sites which carry this material are at
significant risk>.

The net can either tolerate piracy, in which case the sites that do tolerate
it don't have a prayer when they get hauled into court, or the sites on the
net can collectively decide NOT to tolerate information theft.  By taking a
strong stand against the carriage of stolen material you enhance your
chances in the event you get hauled into court, and you help to reduce the 
problem on the net.

Saying that these other cases "weren't piracy related" is nonsense.  They
were <all> about the theft of information (source code, documents, access
codes to systems) that someone else owned, and the net was used in some 
fashion to transport and facilitate the use of the stolen material.

