	For the record, there is probably _far_ more justification for
requesting that an archive at a publicly funded academic institution remove
electronic copies of the Christian Bible, than that they remove Fanny Hill.

	As far as literary works go, if Fanny Hill is removed, then by
extension ALL works dealing with sexual activities should also be
removed--including, of course, such works as Shakespeare's "Rape of Lucerne"
and similar masterpieces.

	
	Censorship, in addition to being an accurate measure of the
narrowness of an individual's mind, is little more than an attempt to impose
one set of values or beliefs on an unaccepting group. As such is is among
the most reprehensible and immoral behaviors imaginable.

	Regardless of whether we view Fanny Hill as outstanding literature
or as the Victorian equivalent of a Harlequin novel, it has as much right to
be archived by an educational institution as does any other work. If
individuals are offended by it, there is a very simple solution. Don't
download it in the first place.

	Of course, as simple as that solution is, it's probably FAR too
complex for the kinds of minds which demand the right to determine what is
best for everyone else! Like suggestions that they control their children's
reading or television viewing, such a concept simply flies in the face of
their "common sense" solution of "if you (or presumably the consummate
censor and religious fascist, Donald Wildmon) don't like it--ban it!"

